Butler: Background Tasks for Webhooks, Cron and the Command Line
****************************************************************

<div class=perex>

Butler is a PHP library for the work your web application does outside of ordinary pages: it downloads new releases from GitHub when a webhook arrives, processes a payment from PayPal, recalculates statistics from cron. You write each such task as one class, and Butler takes care of when, how many times and by whom it runs.

Above all, it solves a trap that tasks triggered by webhooks fall into sooner or later: when a hundred of them arrive at once and each one waits for a lock, they occupy every PHP-FPM process and the whole site stops responding. With Butler, the sender gets an answer within milliseconds, a single process does the task and nothing gets lost.

</div>


Why a Plain Lock Is Not Enough
==============================

A task that must not run twice at the same time usually takes a lock, for example with `flock()`. A second request waits until the first one finishes and only then gets to work. As long as webhooks arrive one at a time, this works without a hitch.

Then you release a new version in forty repositories at once, and GitHub sends a hundred webhooks within a few minutes. Every request waits for the lock, and because it waits, it holds a PHP-FPM process. Soon all of them are taken and the site returns error 504. GitHub and nginx gave up long ago, but PHP keeps waiting. Only a restart helps.

Butler never waits for the lock. A request **records that work is needed, answers, and only then tries the lock**. When another process holds it, the request simply ends: its work is recorded, and the process holding the lock will see it. At any moment at most one process works on a task, and the other requests occupy the server for a few milliseconds only.


Installation
============

```shell
composer require dg/butler
```

Butler requires PHP 8.4 or newer. It needs nothing else, and the bundled [extension |configuration#Nette] connects it to Nette.


The First Task
==============

A task is a class with a single method `run()`:

```php
#[Butler\AllowedLocally]
final class ImportReleasesTask implements Butler\Task
{
	public function run(): void
	{
		// downloads releases from GitHub and updates their list on the site
	}
}
```

The attribute `#[Butler\AllowedLocally]` says the task may run on your machine too. A task without it runs only in production, so that development never sends an e-mail to a customer or charges a payment by mistake. More on that in the chapter [Tasks |tasks#Tasks That Run Only in Production].

Webhooks and cron call a single file, for example `www/endpoint.php`, which looks like this:

```php
$endpoint = new Butler\Endpoint(
	$container->getByType(Butler\Runner::class),
	verifier: new Butler\Verifiers\GitHubSignature($secret),
);

$endpoint->handle([
	'release' => ImportReleasesTask::class,
]);
```

Now you can add a webhook in the repository settings on GitHub pointing to `https://example.org/endpoint.php?release`, with the same secret you have in `$secret`. GitHub gets its answer right away, and the import runs after the answer has been sent. When GitHub sends a hundred webhooks at once, the import runs once or twice, not a hundred times in a row.


What Butler Can Do
==================

- **Two kinds of tasks.** A [Task |tasks] needs no input, and several runs requested at the same time merge into one. A [PayloadTask |tasks#PayloadTask: A Task with Input] gets the body of the request and processes each one separately, in the order they arrived.
- **Verifying the caller.** A GitHub signature, a token in a header or in the URL, or a combination. See [Endpoint |endpoint#Who May Trigger a Task].
- **Nothing gets lost.** A request is written to disk before the endpoint answers and removed only once the task succeeds. When a process dies halfway, the next run takes over the work. See [How It Works |how-it-works].
- **A command line for manual runs and for cron**, including a report of what got stuck. See [Command Line and Cron |cli].
- **Tasks that never reach a developer's machine**, because they run only in production.


When Butler Is Not the Right Choice
===================================

Butler is meant for an application on a single server. The store is a directory on disk and the locks are file locks, so on several servers behind a load balancer each server would watch over its own tasks only.

Nor is it a scheduler. Cron decides what runs when, Butler only makes sure the run is safe. And it is not a full-blown queue with workers like Symfony Messenger. If you need to process thousands of messages a minute, reach for that.

